# ==========================================
# 1. PYTHON BYTECODE & IMPORT CACHES
# Why: Python compiles .py → .pyc for faster imports.
# These are derived from source and platform-dependent.
# ==========================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.pyo

# ==========================================
# 2. VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS
# Why: Dependencies are environment-specific and reproducible
# via manifests (requirements.txt, lockfiles, etc.).
# Keeping them out saves space and avoids hardcoded paths.
# ==========================================
.venv/
venv/
ENV/
env/

# ==========================================
# 3. DISTRIBUTION & BUILD ARTIFACTS
# Why: Generated during packaging. Never commit build outputs.
# ==========================================
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
*.egg
*.whl
.eggs/

# ==========================================
# 4. TESTING, LINTING & TYPE-CHECKING CACHES
# Why: Tool-specific caches that regenerate on each run.
# ==========================================
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pyright_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/

# ==========================================
# 5. IDE & EDITOR WORKSPACE FILES
# Why: Personal/editor preferences, not project logic.
# (Uncomment if your team shares IDE configs)
# ==========================================
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ==========================================
# 6. OS, ENV & MISC
# ==========================================
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# ==========================================
# 7. JUPYTER (if applicable)
# ==========================================
.ipynb_checkpoints/